# Runbook — rollback

The deploy job rolls traffic back by itself when the health check or the customer smoke checks in
`.github/smoke.txt` fail: `scripts/deploy.sh revert` returns traffic to what served before the deploy, and the job
fails. Keep `smoke.txt` to real customer entry paths and the refusals they must return.

For a problem found after the checks pass, roll back through the pipeline: dispatch the release workflow on the last
good release's tag with `rollback=true`. It redeploys the **digest** that release pushed, without building, testing
or migrating, and runs the same health and smoke checks, with the same automatic traffic rollback if they fail.
Nothing is applied from a workstation (safety floor, rule 5).

Migrations are forward-only and are skipped: the older code runs against the newer schema, which works because every
migration keeps the previous release working. If it does not, ship a forward fix instead.

## TL;DR

```sh
gh release list --limit 10                                          # pick the last good tag
gh workflow run release.yml --ref v1.3.0 -f environment=prod -f rollback=true
gh run watch                                                        # pick the new release run
```

Anyone who can dispatch workflows can roll back; the `prod` GitHub environment's protection rules apply as for any
deploy. The run refuses a ref that is not a tag, and a tag whose commit never reached a successful deployment, such as
a pre-release whose dev deploy failed.

The tag's own `release.yml` runs, not `main`'s, so deploy-step fixes made since that release are absent. A tag created
before the release workflow had the `rollback` input cannot be rolled back to this way: pick a newer tag, or ship a
forward fix.

## Symptom → Fix

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Deploy job failed its checks and rolled traffic back | The environment already serves the previous revision. Do NOT re-run the failed release — same code, same failure; fix forward |
| Bad deploy also ran a migration | **Do not roll back the schema.** Forward-only. Roll back the code if it works against the new schema; otherwise ship a forward-fix migration immediately |
| Need to roll back further than the last release | Dispatch the rollback on an older tag from `gh release list`; it must have been released through this pipeline |
| The rollback run fails with "no released image" | The ref was never released here, or its image was deleted from the registry; pick another tag |
| Rolled back, but the *cause* is still in `main` | A rollback fixes the environment, not the bug. File an issue, then fix forward — the next release will redeploy the same broken commit otherwise |

## Out of scope for this playbook

Rolling back infrastructure (Terraform or any other infrastructure code) is not an image redeploy. Revert the change in
a pull request and let the project's infrastructure pipeline apply it; if no pipeline applies it, record that as a gap
in `docs/agreements.md`, with an owner and a date.
